WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:02.042
MARTIN OMANDER: Hi, Tianzi.

00:00:02.042 --> 00:00:03.000
TIANZI CAI: Hi, Martin.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.370
What's going on?

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.120
MARTIN OMANDER: I'm
writing a ride hailing

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.840
app that matches cars
with riders in real time

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.130
using Pub/Sub.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:12.450
I noticed some glitches.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.970
And I wondered if
you could help me.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:18.900
TIANZI CAI: A ride hailing
app, that sounds super cool.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.530
Pub/Sub has released several
new features this year.

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:25.080
I wonder if some
of them could help.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.910
Could you tell me more
about your use cases?

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:29.111
MARTIN OMANDER: Of course.

00:00:29.111 --> 00:00:31.075
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.080
MARTIN OMANDER: When a
rider requests a ride,

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:45.250
the rider's mobile app sends
an HTTP post to a Cloud Run

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:48.040
service called Rider Handler.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.040
That service publishes
a message to Pub/Sub.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.970
The message is consumed
by the Dispatcher service.

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:58.390
The Dispatcher service calls
the Fleet Manager service

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:00.220
to find nearby drivers.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:02.170
It then sends a
push notification

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:05.680
to the nearest driver via
Firebase Cloud Messaging.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.710
When a driver accepts
or declines a ride,

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:10.330
their mobile app
calls a cloud run

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:12.940
service called Driver Handler.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.700
That service publishes
a message to Pub/Sub,

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.780
which is consumed by the
Fleet Manager service.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.430
Pub/Sub lets me
decouple Rider Handler

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:25.700
from Dispatcher and Driver
Handler from Fleet Manager.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:28.960
It also lets me add more message
subscribers in the future,

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.030
for example, for reporting.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.430
TIANZI CAI: Thank you for
showing me how the app works.

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:35.950
I see how Pub/Sub fits in now.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.150
Let's see the issues you face.

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:40.150
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, the
first issue I'm facing

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:44.710
is caused by malformed GPS
data from riders' phones.

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:47.350
It's still a little bit of a
mystery to me how this happens,

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:51.970
but when a dispatcher receives
Pub/Sub messages with bad GPS

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:54.490
data, it throws an
exception and doesn't

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:56.590
acknowledge the message.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:58.810
When there are many
such bad messages,

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:02.830
new ride requests don't
make it to the dispatcher.

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:04.180
TIANZI CAI: Oh, no.

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.730
It sounds like
you've got a growing

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:12.420
backlog of undeliverable
messages in your Pub/Sub topic.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:16.590
Could you show me the
graphs for unacked message

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:20.095
count and oldest
unacked message?

00:02:20.095 --> 00:02:20.970
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:24.030
I went into the Pub/Sub
area of Cloud Console

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.040
and I took these screenshots.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.030
TIANZI CAI: Ah.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:29.350
This confirms my suspicion.

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:30.840
Let me explain.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.210
A small backlog is harmless.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:36.630
But a huge backlog could
cause all your messages

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:39.330
to be stuck because
Pub/Sub always

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:43.110
tries to deliver the
oldest messages first.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:46.800
You never want a
situation where the number

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.730
of undelivered messages grows
with the age of the oldest

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:52.180
message.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:54.790
It looks like you have
that kind of backup.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.400
And it's causing
new messages to be

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.848
stuck behind the old messages.

00:02:59.848 --> 00:03:00.640
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:01.600
I see.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:03.130
What should I do?

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:05.110
TIANZI CAI: You should
use a [INAUDIBLE] cue.

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:07.150
You could tell
Pub/Sub to forward

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:10.900
the undeliverable messages
to a different topic,

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.750
also known as a
dead letter topic,

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:18.550
after a certain number of
failed delivery attempts.

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:19.900
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, really?

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.900
Can I update an
existing subscription

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:25.606
to send bad messages
to a dead letter queue?

00:03:25.606 --> 00:03:26.830
TIANZI CAI: Absolutely.

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:29.420
Let's go to Cloud
Console to do that.

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:32.110
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:35.590
TIANZI CAI: Let's go to your
subscription dispatcher-sub.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:38.120
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:40.185
TIANZI CAI: Edit it.

00:03:40.185 --> 00:03:41.060
MARTIN OMANDER: Edit.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:42.710
Yep.

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:45.560
TIANZI CAI: We want to
scroll down to Dead Lettering

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:47.702
and enable Dead Lettering.

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:54.660
MARTIN OMANDER:
This checkbox here?

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:55.702
TIANZI CAI: That's right.

00:03:59.339 --> 00:04:00.756
MARTIN OMANDER:
Dead Letter Topic.

00:04:00.756 --> 00:04:02.089
TIANZI CAI: You want to create--

00:04:03.367 --> 00:04:04.450
MARTIN OMANDER: You said--

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:05.117
TIANZI CAI: Yes.

00:04:05.117 --> 00:04:08.140
Create a Dead Letter topic
to forward the messages.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:09.970
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:12.400
So I'll call that
riders-dead-letter-topic.

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:20.730
TIANZI CAI: You also want to
attach a default subscription

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:23.140
to your dead letter topic.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:25.200
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:33.070
I'll call that
riders-dead-letter-subscription.

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:38.900
TIANZI CAI: Sounds great.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:42.020
How many times do you want
Pub/Sub to try before it

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.670
forwards the bad messages?

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:46.670
MARTIN OMANDER: So
this is how many times

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:49.310
it will have to fail
in dispatcher before it

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:52.550
comes into this
subscription, is that right?

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:53.690
TIANZI CAI: That's correct.

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:54.680
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:55.970
Maybe 10 times.

00:04:55.970 --> 00:04:58.300
If it doesn't work after
10, it will never work.

00:05:00.383 --> 00:05:01.925
TIANZI CAI: Now you
can click Update.

00:05:04.187 --> 00:05:05.270
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:12.698
TIANZI CAI: And you want
to go to Dead Lettering.

00:05:12.698 --> 00:05:13.740
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, yeah.

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:15.830
There's an orange
triangle there.

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:16.380
Oh.

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.920
TIANZI CAI: This is to
grant Pub/Sub the publisher

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:23.020
and subscriber role so
that it could forward

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:25.317
the messages on your behalf.

00:05:25.317 --> 00:05:26.400
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, I see.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.550
So I just granted these roles.

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:30.900
TIANZI CAI: Yes.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:33.270
You will expect
messages that exceeds

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:36.450
the maximum number
of delivery attempts

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:39.570
to be forwarded to
your Dead Letter topic.

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:40.950
MARTIN OMANDER: Excellent.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:44.130
I should ask for someone who
knows more about GPS data

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:47.010
to write a service that
subscribes to that Dead Letter

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.950
subscription and do
a full diagnosis.

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:52.410
TIANZI CAI: They will have
all the bad data in one place.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:55.110
I think they will
appreciate that.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:57.720
MARTIN OMANDER: The second
issue I have is mistakenly

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:01.650
sending cars to pick up
a rider who has canceled.

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:05.910
First, the rider sends
a request ride message.

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:09.090
Next, the rider sends
a canceled ride message

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:10.770
if they want to cancel the ride.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:14.220
And for some reason, the
canceled ride message sometimes

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:15.870
arrives first.

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:18.510
When it does, the dispatcher
doesn't know about this ride

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:20.460
yet, so it can't cancel it.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:23.730
And then later the request
ride message comes in.

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:26.070
And the dispatcher
then creates the rides

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:29.400
and finds a driver for
this canceled ride.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:33.330
TIANZI CAI: This sounds like
messages were definitely

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:35.440
arriving out of order.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:38.680
But Pub/Sub Message
Ordering could help.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:39.930
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, excellent.

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:42.190
Please tell me more.

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:46.210
TIANZI CAI: I imagine
requests matter per rider.

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:49.300
When these requests are
published to Pub/Sub,

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:54.220
if you attach the rider's
ID as an ordering key,

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:57.820
then these messages will be
delivered in the same order

00:06:57.820 --> 00:06:59.980
they are received by Pub/Sub.

00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:03.370
The request ride message will
arrive in a dispatcher service

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:06.307
first, followed by the
cancel ride message.

00:07:06.307 --> 00:07:08.140
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, and
this would make sure

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:10.300
that the dispatcher
is always using

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:14.940
the latest, correct information
about each ride, right?

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:16.020
TIANZI CAI: Exactly.

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:17.687
MARTIN OMANDER: I
want to give it a try.

00:07:17.687 --> 00:07:19.110
How do I set it up, Tianzi?

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:21.867
TIANZI CAI: To set it up, let's
go to Cloud Console again.

00:07:21.867 --> 00:07:22.950
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:23.790
I'm here.

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:26.610
I go into Subscriptions.

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:29.730
TIANZI CAI: Let's
recreate a dispatcher sub

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:34.460
so that it has ordering enabled.

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:36.510
MARTIN OMANDER: So I
start by deleting it.

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:37.970
Is that right?

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:39.110
TIANZI CAI: That's right.

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:40.193
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:44.764
Delete.

00:07:44.764 --> 00:07:48.320
Then I'll create a new
or replacement for it.

00:07:55.130 --> 00:07:57.980
So I'll call it
dispatcher-sub-ordered

00:07:57.980 --> 00:07:58.480
because--

00:07:58.480 --> 00:07:59.840
TIANZI CAI: It sounds great.

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:00.970
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah.

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:03.040
And it's the rider's topic.

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:07.000
It's push because we're using
Cloud Run on the other side.

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:12.100
And this is my
Cloud Run end point.

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:14.410
And I'm just recreating
what I'd done

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:15.800
on the previous subscription.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:17.560
So I enabled
authentication for it.

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:20.210
I set up an account for that.

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:22.690
And then there was something
I should do differently.

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:25.953
Ordering?

00:08:25.953 --> 00:08:26.620
TIANZI CAI: Yes.

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:27.953
MARTIN OMANDER: Is this it?

00:08:27.953 --> 00:08:28.620
TIANZI CAI: Yes.

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:30.937
You want to enable
message ordering.

00:08:30.937 --> 00:08:32.020
MARTIN OMANDER: Very good.

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:33.653
And that's it?

00:08:33.653 --> 00:08:34.570
TIANZI CAI: That's it.

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:37.867
You can click Create.

00:08:37.867 --> 00:08:38.950
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:40.690
TIANZI CAI: And in
the meantime, I'm

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:44.500
going to publish a
request ride message

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:49.447
from Bobcat followed by a
cancel ride message from Bobcat.

00:08:49.447 --> 00:08:50.530
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:55.000
I'm selecting the log for
dispatcher service here.

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:04.535
And there I see a
request ride from Bobcat.

00:09:04.535 --> 00:09:05.660
And let's see what is this.

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:06.750
TIANZI CAI: Yeah.

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:07.715
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah.

00:09:07.715 --> 00:09:09.920
And the cancel ride from Bobcat.

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:12.020
Our drivers will
appreciate this.

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:14.660
They came in in the right order.

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:16.215
TIANZI CAI: That's
great to hear.

00:09:16.215 --> 00:09:17.090
MARTIN OMANDER: Cool.

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:20.540
So with Dead Letter queues
and message ordering enabled,

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:22.730
I'm eager to test
out a new version

00:09:22.730 --> 00:09:25.310
of the algorithm in
the dispatcher that

00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:28.400
matches riders and drivers.

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:29.450
TIANZI CAI: Definitely.

00:09:29.450 --> 00:09:33.560
It will be cool to compare
the algorithms side by side.

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:36.680
I'm thinking, if you
can replay the messages,

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:38.960
you can compare
the new algorithm

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:40.395
with the old algorithm.

00:09:40.395 --> 00:09:41.270
MARTIN OMANDER: Cool.

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:42.830
That sounds fantastic.

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:46.670
But, Tia, can you explain
what replay means?

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:48.680
TIANZI CAI: To
replay messages, you

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:52.760
need to create a Snapshot
on a given subscription.

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:55.160
Another way to think
about a snapshot

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:58.628
is to consider it a
recording that you start.

00:09:58.628 --> 00:09:59.420
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:02.870
I have a subscription which
hits the production dispatcher.

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:04.190
We just saw it.

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:07.880
If I want to replay messages
to my test dispatcher

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:11.100
and compare it with the one
in production, can I do that?

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:12.590
TIANZI CAI: Absolutely.

00:10:12.590 --> 00:10:15.497
Let's head over to Cloud
Console to do that.

00:10:15.497 --> 00:10:16.580
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:19.010
Let me just first
grab the address

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:22.070
